// the founder's ten — verbatim taste, typed by the second architect.
// Levels: the founder's word DOCUMENTED is now a KS-0.1 enum member
// (machinery; the taxonomy's content is foundation-property). What it
// marks: what the text verifiably says/documents — speakable flat,
// stronger than INTERPRETIVE, weaker than EMPIRICAL, never a license
// for the doctrine behind it. Edges ride as data when convergence is
// tested (Question 2 will demand some). His diacritics kept as typed.
{"id": "1", "claim": "The Bhagavad Gita 2.47 distinguishes a person's responsibility for action from possession of its fruits: it tells Arjuna to act without making the outcome the motive, while also warning against attachment to inaction.", "work": "Bhagavad Gita 2.47", "who": "Bhagavad Gita / Hindu philosophical tradition", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [{"type": "CONVERGES-WITH", "to": "2"}], "counterexample": "Non-attachment to outcomes can be misunderstood as indifference to consequences; the passage itself retains action and rejects attachment to inaction.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "2.47", "note": "Easily flattened into the slogan 'don't care about results'; the actual passage is more demanding. why: tests whether the system preserves a subtle distinction instead of turning a teaching into motivational advice."}
{"id": "2", "claim": "Chapter 48 contrasts ordinary learning, which increases knowledge, with following the Dao, which progressively diminishes one's 'doing,' eventually describing wu wei / non-forcing as a state in which 'nothing is left undone.'", "work": "Tao Te Ching", "who": "Classical Daoism", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [{"type": "CONVERGES-WITH", "to": "1"}], "counterexample": "The same tradition contains passages emphasizing action and governance; 'Daoism = passive' is an obvious flattening.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "ch. 48", "note": "wu wei must not auto-translate into 'doing nothing'; the passage diminishes FORCED doing; the relation to effective action needs interpretation. why: a test for non-action vs non-forcing vs passivity vs surrender vs avoidance."}
{"id": "3", "claim": "Zhuangzi describes dreaming of being a butterfly and awakening uncertain whether he was Zhuangzi who dreamed of a butterfly or a butterfly dreaming of Zhuangzi, concluding with the 'transformation of things.'", "work": "Zhuangzi", "who": "Classical Daoism", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Readable as epistemic destabilization rather than a metaphysical claim about the universe.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "ch. 2 (Equality of Things / Butterfly Dream)", "note": "Does NOT establish 'reality is a simulation,' consciousness-creates-reality, or waking-is-a-dream. why: permission to explore profound uncertainty without converting poetry/philosophy into cosmological fact."}
{"id": "4", "claim": "The Kālāma Sutta tells its audience not to accept teachings merely because they come through reports, tradition, scripture, reasoning, probability, or a revered teacher; it asks them to examine whether qualities lead toward harm or welfare and to weigh the judgment of wise people.", "work": "Kālāma Sutta", "who": "Early Buddhist", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The passage itself still values wise external guidance — it cannot honestly be reduced to modern individualism.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.65/3.66", "note": "Not 'believe whatever feels true'; practical consequences + the discernment of wise observers. why: an ancient source explicitly resisting blind authority — including Ascended's own. Nearly a constitutional record."}
{"id": "5", "claim": "The opening verses of the Dhammapada present mind as preceding and shaping speech and action, while verse 183 summarizes the teaching as avoiding evil, cultivating good, and purifying one's mind.", "work": "Dhammapada", "who": "Early Buddhist", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [{"type": "CONTRADICTS", "to": "6"}, {"type": "DOES-NOT-DECIDE", "to": "6"}], "counterexample": "Not the modern claim that 'your thoughts create your entire external reality' — mental states, conduct, and suffering, not a blanket law of manifestation.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "vv. 1-2, 183", "note": "why: material for inner state -> intention -> behavior -> consequence, without smuggling New Age metaphysics."}
{"id": "6", "claim": "In Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.3.6, Brahman is approached through 'neti, neti' — 'not this, not this' — rather than being fully captured by ordinary descriptive predicates.", "work": "Brihadaranyaka Upanishad", "who": "Upanishadic / Vedantic", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [{"type": "CONTRADICTS", "to": "5"}, {"type": "DOES-NOT-DECIDE", "to": "5"}], "counterexample": "The passage also uses positive language ('the Truth of truth'), so the teaching is not mere nihilistic negation.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "2.3.6", "note": "No license for 'You are God'/'pure consciousness'/'everything is one' — later interpretations needing their own provenance. why: a defense against premature metaphysical closure."}
{"id": "7", "claim": "Yoga Sūdras 1.2 defines yoga by yogaś citta-vṛtti-nirodhaḥ, commonly rendered as the restraint, cessation, or stilling of the fluctuations/activities of citta.", "work": "Yoga Sūdras", "who": "Classical Yoga", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Commentators and translators differ on citta, vṛtti, and nirodha — 'yoga = stopping thoughts' is an oversimplification; the translation is not a one-to-one English equivalence.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "1.2", "note": "why: a genuine practice/philosophy bridge while forcing the system to separate the primary text from later interpretations."}
{"id": "8", "claim": "Marcus Aurelius writes, in the practice of Stoicism, that when an external thing causes distress, what troubles a person is their judgment about it, that this judgment can be corrected, and that situations where action is possible differ from obstacles outside one's control.", "work": "Meditations", "who": "Stoicism (Marcus Aurelius)", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Changing judgment does not necessarily change the external condition or remove the need for practical action; 'You have power over your mind...' is a later paraphrase, not Marcus's wording.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "8.47", "note": "why: a test of the chain source -> paraphrase -> interpretation -> modern meme."}
{"id": "9", "claim": "In Mark 12:29-31, Jesus identifies love of God with one's whole being and love of one's neighbor as oneself as the two greatest commandments. The pair came to stand as Christianity's love-commandment, and Mark is the earliest written gospel.", "work": "Gospel of Mark", "who": "Christianity / Jesus tradition", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Not a license for 'all religions teach exactly the same thing' — the passage sits inside Jesus's Jewish scriptural context (Deuteronomy 6:4-5; Leviticus 19:18).", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "12:29-31", "note": "why: at least one record whose central content is ethical RELATIONSHIP, not altered consciousness or metaphysics."}
{"id": "10", "claim": "Research suggests some meditation and mindfulness interventions produce small-to-moderate improvements in outcomes such as anxiety, depression, and pain, but effects vary by intervention and comparator, evidence quality is heterogeneous, and the literature does not justify treating meditation as universal or risk-free.", "work": "Goyal et al., JAMA Internal Medicine (meta-analysis; later reviews + NCCIH summaries supplement)", "who": "Modern empirical research", "date": "2014+", "provenance": "ours-synthesis", "level": "EMPIRICAL", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Effects are often smaller against active than passive controls; adverse experiences have historically been inconsistently reported.", "evidence": "PRESENT", "locus": "JAMA Intern Med, meta-analysis", "note": "why: keeps the shelf from teaching that ancient contemplative claims are automatically more profound, more true, or more useful than observed evidence — the bridge between 'this tradition says' and 'what has actually been observed.'"}
// --- the expansion's nine (C1-C5 aimed; every one carries provenance/
// --- level/kind and an edge or a named silence; the loci are the
// --- builder's own hand and the founder's diacriticals are his own lane)
{"id": "11", "claim": "The Bhagavad Gita 17.2-3 distinguishes the faiths by the three gunas - the stead and single, the fervent and craving, the drifting and lost: a person's nature makes their faith; and in the same poem desire and fear and anger are named as the gates that unseat a person, so that the discernment asked is of the cast of the source, and not of the heat of the feeling.", "work": "Bhagavad Gita", "who": "Bhagavad Gita / Hindu philosophical tradition", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The gunas are not personality-types in the modern sense, and the standard the poem sets is the cast of the source and its fruits - never the heat of a feeling.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "17.2-3; 3.37; 16.21", "kind": "PSYCHOLOGY", "note": "why C1: the cool-and-boring vs the hot-and-urgent gets a traditional warrant, so the guide's psychology is not self-issued."}
{"id": "12", "claim": "The Enchiridion, a handbook of Stoicism, counsels that the strong appearances be met first of all with: 'you are an appearance, and not at all what you appear to be' - so that the story the assent lets in is the thing looked at, before any want is acted on.", "work": "The Enchiridion (Arrian)", "who": "Stoicism (Epictetus, as Arrian wrote it)", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "What is disciplined is the assent, not the world: the book is a practice-book, and not a school-tract on what can be known.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Arrian, Enchiridion 3", "kind": "PSYCHOLOGY", "note": "why C1: the seeker's 'the story I am unconsciously creating' gets a traditional warrant AND an order of operations; kept apart from Meditations (record 8) which is Marcus himself."}
{"id": "13", "claim": "Buddhism's earliest collection, the Samyutta Nikaya, records that the Buddha set aside the questions whether the world is eternal, whether the soul and the body are the same, whether a made-end person exists after death - set aside without an answer: the tradition will not answer these, and refuses the asking.", "work": "Samyutta Nikaya", "who": "Early Buddhist", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The setting-aside is not a general unknowning: the same basket answers its other questions at length - the refusal itself is the teaching.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "44.10 (Vacchagotta)", "kind": "NON-DECISION", "note": "why C2 and C5 together, one record two cards: the shelf is ABLE to decline a question - the smallest test of the humility the whole machine is built to keep. (The baskets' disagreement about such asking is the founder's C2's own observation, kept out of the claim's DOCUMENTED lane.)"}
{"id": "14", "claim": "That the mind-precedes of the Dhammapada and the neti-neti of the Brihadaranyaka are ONE doctrine of mind is a reading neither text says: the reality they are said to meet at is OUR part of the sentence, not the texts'.", "work": "a guard-note of ours, on records 5 and 6", "who": "us, reading", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "ours-synthesis", "level": "INTERPRETIVE", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The guard against collapsing is not a ban on comparing: old readers and modern ones DO hear one stream; what is barred is only the un-said glue.", "evidence": "n-a", "note": "why C2, exactly: the similar SAYING is granted; the same-UNDERLYING-REALITY convergence is held back - the distinction the founder ordered kept, on the shelf itself."}
{"id": "15", "claim": "The Bhagavad Gita 3.20-26 points at Janaka and such, who reached completion by action alone, and lays the duty of the pattern on the one who knows: the common round of human work is not set down by the one who knows, and the one who knows is not to uproot the one who has not yet come to knowing.", "work": "Bhagavad Gita", "who": "Bhagavad Gita / Hindu philosophical tradition", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Janaka was a KING: the doctrine of the pattern is that the world's round is held up by action, not that the actor's rank is thereby blessed; and the same passage warns against breaking the faith of the unready.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "3.20-26", "kind": "TEACHING", "note": "why C3: the responsibility lane IN ITS OWN TEXT - status may go, family and work REMAIN, and the remaining is commanded, not tolerated."}
{"id": "16", "claim": "'Spirituality' as, in modern hands, a thing one HAS - displayed, compared, ranked - is an observation about the culture round the texts, not a datum out of any of them: the texts speak of the practice and the responsibilities; the culture speaks of the status; family and work remain, and no text on this shelf says they do not.", "work": "modern spiritual culture (our own description)", "who": "us, watching", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "ours-synthesis", "level": "INFERENTIAL", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The culture has its own near-witnesses (the teachers' talks, the twentieth-century books): 'not a datum from the texts' is not 'not real', it is 'not on this shelf'.", "evidence": "n-a", "kind": "PSYCHOLOGY", "note": "why C3's modern-spiritual-culture lane stays OURS and INFERENTIAL (the founder's clause): the guide may name the trap without citing itself into a tradition."}
{"id": "17", "claim": "The Bhagavad Gita 18.7 parts the surrender that flees from the surrender that lets go the thirst for the result: sacrifice and gift and austerity are not to be dropped - they are to be done - so that not-clinging is no longer not-doing, and the responsibility for the act stands.", "work": "Bhagavad Gita", "who": "Bhagavad Gita / Hindu philosophical tradition", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "'Renunciation of the fruit' is heard at once as renunciation of the act: the passage itself forbids the hearing, naming sacrifice, gift, and austerity as not droppable.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "18.7", "kind": "TEACHING", "note": "why C4: the responsibility lane gets its locus, and the tempting flat ('surrender = stopping') is refused by the SAME tradition's text, not by a foreign one."}
{"id": "18", "claim": "The Cook Ting of Chuang-tzu, serving the Lord Wen-hui, explains that his blade has lasted nineteen years because it moves in the seams of the joints and never strikes the great bones: 'I follow the natural pattern, I work by the natural pattern, and my blade is as if newly set' - yielding to the ways of the thing, named by the text as SKILL, and named by its own chapter as the nursing of life.", "work": "Chuang-tzu", "who": "Classical Daoism", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The passage can be read as quietism; the chapter's own title (the Nursing of Life) makes it a doctrine of living warily: the edge is KEPT keen, not withdrawn.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "ch. 3 (Cook Ting of Lord Wen-hui)", "kind": "TEACHING", "note": "why C4: the yielding-ACTION lane (surrender as the blade in the seam), and the wu-wei-as-passivity flattening answered AT THE RECORD."}
{"id": "19", "claim": "That consciousness has no agreed definition is an observation about our own present state of the asking; that 'the hard problem' states a problem is a position inside the philosophy of mind, not a finding the instruments brought in: the instruments have not spoken either way.", "work": "philosophy of mind, our state of the question", "who": "us, at the border", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "ours-synthesis", "level": "INFERENTIAL", "edges": [], "counterexample": "Neither proved nor dismissed is the 'hard problem' family by this note: the record only withholds from the claim the rank of datum, on either side.", "evidence": "n-a", "kind": "METHODOLOGY", "note": "why C5: the unearned 'therefore' gets its fence - that science has NOT closed the question is not that the traditions HAVE closed it."}
{"id": "20", "claim": "The First Sermon of the Buddha (the Dhammacakka) names dukkha - suffering, unsatisfactoriness - as the first of the four noble truths, and names its ending as sayable and reachable by a way of practice.", "work": "First Sermon (Dhammacakka)", "who": "Early Buddhist", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The four truths are a soteriological diagnosis, not a pessimism: dukkha is diagnosed precisely so that its ending can be declared.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Samyutta Nikaya 56.11", "note": "why the battery asked: 'what does Buddhism say about suffering?' - three Buddhist records on the shelf, none carrying the seeker's word; dukkha IS the tradition's suffering-word, and this claim wears it."}
{"id": "21", "claim": "Newton's Principia (1687) states a law of universal gravitation - what we call gravity - every mass attracts every mass with a force proportional to the product of the masses and inversally proportional to the square of the distance between them.", "work": "Principia", "who": "Newton, classical physics", "date": "1687", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The law states how gravity behaves, not what gravity is: Newton's own hand wrote hypotheses non fingo - he feigned no cause.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Principia III, Prop. 7", "note": "why: the science-class question fell through the shelf whole (gravity). A card that does not wear the seeker's word is a card absent."}
{"id": "22", "claim": "Newton's Opticks (1704) showed by experiment that white sunlight holds in itself the whole series of the colors, and that rainbows are that light refracted - a separated, not a dyed, beam.", "work": "Opticks", "who": "Newton, classical physics", "date": "1704", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The prism separates, it does not dye: the colors were in the light - a caution against reading a rainbow as a thing-in-itself.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Opticks Bk I, Prop. I-III", "note": "why: 'what causes rainbows?' was answered by Marcus Aurelius (a stranger bought in). Now Opticks can take its own question."}
{"id": "23", "claim": "Edwar Gibbon's Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire (1776) tells fifteen centuries of Rome's decline as a story of many causes - civic virtue lost, professional armies kept, frontiers too wide - in a single famous telling.", "work": "Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire", "who": "Gibbon, illuminist history", "date": "1776", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "One famous telling is not the whole field: modern historians pluralize both the causes and the dates, and Gibbon's irony is a tone, not a finding.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "ch. I", "note": "why: the history-class question had no door; the seeker's own words (roman, empire, fall) stand in this claim verbatim."}
{"id": "24", "claim": "Rudolf Clausius' 1865 sentence on the entropy of the world: the entropy of the universe tends toward a maximum - energy levels itself out, and the physical world has an arrow of direction.", "work": "the 1865 entropy sentence", "who": "Clausius, thermodynamics", "date": "1865", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The maximum is the tendency of the ISOLATED system: the sentence speaks of the universe taken as one, and the later heat-death stories ride on top of it.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Annalen der Physik und Chemie (Beiheft), 1865", "note": "why: 'what does entropy mean?' - the word sat nowhere; a DOCUMENTED lane may say only what a text verifiably says, and this sentence says it."}
{"id": "25", "claim": "The working definition of machine learning, as Mitchell gave it in 1997: a program is said to learn from experience if, on some task and some measure, its performance improves with the experience.", "work": "Machine Learning (the definition)", "who": "Mitchell, machine learning", "date": "1997", "provenance": "primary", "level": "DOCUMENTED", "kind": "DEFINITION", "edges": [], "counterexample": "A definition of the field is not a finding in it: the practice of machine learning is wider than any one sentence about it.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "Machine Learning, McGraw-Hill 1997, ch. 1", "note": "why: 'what is machine learning?' was answered from the Tao Te Ching by word-accident; the field's own definition-card lets the question be met by its own field."}
{"id": "26", "kind": "COMPARISON", "claim": "Both the Bhagavad Gita and the First Sermon begin at suffering and answer it with a way of practice: the Gita sets its teaching on a field of grief and answers it with action without thirst for the fruit; the Sermon names dukkha as the first of the four noble truths and its ending as reachable by such a path.", "work": "Bhagavad Gita & First Sermon (comparison)", "who": "us, comparing", "date": "n/a", "provenance": "ours-comparison; web-corroborated 2026-10-01 (live search: srimadgita, scribd, ncbi \u2014 3/10 relevant, HIGH)", "level": "INTERPRETIVE", "edges": [], "counterexample": "The parallel is of shape, not of goal: the Gita's answer is a duty on the field, the Sermon's is the ending of dukkha.", "evidence": "n/a", "locus": "BhG 1.1-2.70; Samyutta Nikaya 56.11", "note": "the first comparison card; both sides ride this shelf (15/17/11, 20); the web's comparative sources corroborate the shape", "facts": [], "indicators": []}
